 biden six  seven months ago that the u.s shouldn't  withdraw until there was a stable  settlement and that should have included  conditionality ensuring that the taliban  if they were to be part of the  government would respect human rights  and that so clearly isn't happening so  those conversations did not take place  as far as we know those conversations  did not take place and the government's  response has consistently appeared to be  we're waiting to see what the united  states is going to do  rather than saying we are telling the  united states that it is vital that we  remain in to secure the peace and  stability of the country  where we have been present for the last  20 years  what about the planning for this  post-withdrawal period the defense  secretary said earlier this week that  the operation was always planned it has  had to be  brought forward  if this was a planned operation it's a  chaotic one isn't it  absolutely  there are so many people  in afghanistan who are fearing for their  lives and  we've known about that for months again  it shouldn't have come as a surprise to  the government people have been lobbying  on behalf of the interpreters on behalf  of locally engaged british council staff  on behalf of journalists for weeks and  months  and the government hasn't been listening  they haven't  sought out those people whose lives are  at risk at a time when they could have  done so now in kabul is so much harder  than it would have been three four weeks  ago far less three four months ago  what about the foreign secretary's  decision to take a holiday abroad at the  time of the the us withdrawal top civil  servants at the foreign office the home  office and the ministry of defense still  on holiday according to reports today  all adding to the feeling that we have  abandoned the people of afghanistan in  their hour of need  that's how it looks  that whatever was going through the  foreign secretary's mind when he went on  holiday as soon as he